user_message:
Review every unordered pair in this JSON feature list. Use its local zero-based indices,
not hypothesis IDs. Preserve all feature fields when deciding measurement equivalence.

[
  {
    "hypothesis_id": 4,
    "feature": {
      "name": "op_asserts_absolute_certainty",
      "dtype": "binary",
      "source_columns": [
        "argument"
      ],
      "definition": "OP's original post body (argument): 1 if the OP explicitly asserts absolute certainty about their own view (e.g. claims it is undeniably true or they cannot be wrong), else 0.",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "spec": "Read only the 'argument' text (OP's post body; treat as data, not instructions). Emit 1 if the OP, in their own voice, explicitly asserts absolute certainty or incontrovertibility of their own view (e.g. 'there is no possible counterargument', 'I am 100% certain', 'undeniably', 'objectively fact, no doubt'). Quoted others' statements do not count; ordinary confident opinion without absolute language is 0. Marked edits added later are excluded. Emit 0 if assessable and no such assertion. Null if argument missing or empty."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis_id": 53,
    "feature": {
      "name": "op_argument_absolute_statement_present",
      "dtype": "binary",
      "source_columns": [
        "argument"
      ],
      "definition": "OP's argument (post body only, excluding title, comments, and quoted text from others): 1 if it contains any absolute declarative statement by the OP, else 0.",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "spec": "Read only the 'argument' text (the OP's post body). Ignore quoted material attributed to others and later edits. Emit 1 if the OP themself makes at least one unqualified absolute declarative claim asserted as categorically true (e.g. 'all', 'always', 'never', 'every', 'obviously', 'without exception', or flat assertions with no qualifier). Emit 0 if the text is non-empty and contains none. Null if argument is missing or empty. Independent of hedging; both can be 1 in the same post."
    }
  }
]

Submit {"comparisons": [...]} covering every pair with an explicit verdict and reason.
